<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Theme of Sicily</h1><p class="st-meta">690 CE – 965 CE (approx.) · capital Syracuse</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Byzantine military province of Sicily, based at Syracuse, which slowly fell to Arab conquest between 827 and 965.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> was part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> after Justinian's reconquest in 535. In the late 7th century it was organised as a theme, a province under a military governor (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a>) at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syracuse">Syracuse</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constans-ii">Emperor Constans II</a> had resided at Syracuse from 663 until his murder there in 668.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> commanded the island's forces and also controlled Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> was a rich source of grain and tax revenue, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> owned large estates there until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-iii-the-isaurian">Leo III</a> confiscated them in the 730s. The island became increasingly Greek-speaking and Greek in its church, receiving monks and refugees from the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 827 the rebel commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphemius">Euphemius</a> invited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids of Ifriqiya</a>, who invaded and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> in 831, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syracuse">Syracuse</a> in 878 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Taormina</a> in 902. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rometta">Rometta</a>, the last Byzantine stronghold, fell in 965, completing the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/theme-of-sicily.jpg" alt="Theme of Sicily" width="320" height="146"><figcaption>Image: Amonixinator; derivative work by Hoodinski · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ByzantineEmpire717%2Bextrainfo%2Bthemes.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>690 CE – 965 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Syracuse</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chalcedonian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Byzantine Sicily</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=828&amp;focus=theme-of-sicily" aria-label="Theme of Sicily on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5063 843 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Theme of Sicily lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5383 1099L5709 970M5383 1099L5333 1064"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5333 1064h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5383 1099h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5383 1099h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Syracuse. <a href="/atlas?year=828&amp;focus=theme-of-sicily">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

